# tool: Collections

Data an agent shapes without a programmer: a schema, and the rows that
follow it.

A collection is a declaration — its fields, their types, what a hook
normalises on write — and its records are the rows that declaration governs.
"build me a CRM" becomes a collections_create for "contacts" plus the records
an agent or a person files into it, nothing generated, nothing deployed.

## Commands
- **list** — every collection declared in the workspace, native and dynamic
- **get** — one declaration, including a skill-scoped one (pass skill)
- **create** — declare a new collection
- **delete** — remove a declaration and unregister it
- **records-list** — the rows of one collection
- **records-get** — one row
- **records-create** — file a new row
- **records-update** — revalidate and rewrite a row
- **records-delete** — remove a row

## When to use
- **Structured data with no fixed shape in this codebase:** declare a
  collection before inventing a file format for it
- **A skill's own collection:** list returns it with its Skill field; get and
  delete need that field back to resolve it

## When NOT to use
- Not for anything already native (tasks, agents, memories) — those have
  their own groups and their own validation
- Not for one-off, unstructured content — that is a file

Composite tool `Collections` with 9 actions: create, delete, get, list, records-create, records-delete, records-get, records-list, records-update.

A record is validated against its collection's schema on every write, not
only when it looks wrong: a hook runs before validation, so what fails or
passes is what was actually stored, never what was typed before the hook
touched it.

A skill-scoped collection is removed automatically when its skill is
uninstalled — deleting it directly, by hand, is rarely what you want while
the skill is still installed.

## Usage
Call as `Collections({ action: "<action>", input: { ... }, _reasoning: "..." })`.
Set `schema: true` on the same level as `action` to receive the full action detail (description, examples, input schema, token estimate) instead of executing.

## actions

### collections_create

Declare a new collection.

Declare a table: its fields and, optionally, the hooks that normalise a
record before it is validated.

The schema is checked for internal coherence — every ref points at something
real, every enum has values, no field is named twice — before anything is
written, and the collection is registered only once the write to disk has
actually succeeded.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "id": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Identifier for the collection. Also its directory name: lowercase, digits, hyphen and underscore only."
    },
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Human name of the collection. Example: \"Contacts\"."
    },
    "description": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "What this collection is for."
    },
    "scope": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "workspace or skill. Defaults to workspace."
    },
    "skill": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The skill this collection ships with, when Scope is skill."
    },
    "format": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "md or json — how each record of this collection is laid out on disk."
    },
    "fields": {
      "type": [
        "null",
        "array"
      ],
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "name": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Name of the field. How a record refers to it."
          },
          "type": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One of: string, number, boolean, date, enum, ref, list."
          },
          "description": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "What this field holds, for whoever fills it in."
          },
          "required": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Whether a record must give this field a value."
          },
          "enum": {
            "type": [
              "null",
              "array"
            ],
            "items": {
              "type": "string"
            },
            "description": "The allowed values, for a field of type enum."
          },
          "ref": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The id of another collection, for a field of type ref."
          },
          "default": {
            "description": "Value used when a create hook of action defaultTo applies."
          },
          "unique": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "Whether two records of this collection may share this value."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "name",
          "type"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false
      },
      "description": "The declared columns of a record. At least one is required."
    },
    "hooks": {
      "type": [
        "null",
        "array"
      ],
      "items": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "on": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "When this hook runs: create or update."
          },
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One of: setTimestamp, slugify, defaultTo, computeFrom."
          },
          "field": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The field this hook writes."
          },
          "from": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Source field, for slugify and computeFrom."
          },
          "value": {
            "description": "The default value, for defaultTo."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "on",
          "action",
          "field"
        ],
        "additionalProperties": false
      },
      "description": "Declarative normalisations applied to a record on create or update."
    },
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "id",
    "name",
    "format",
    "fields",
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

### collections_delete

Remove a collection's declaration.

Remove a declaration and unregister it. Its records go with it — nothing
can resolve a write against a declaration that no longer exists.

A skill-scoped collection is normally removed by uninstalling the skill, not
by calling this directly while the skill is still installed.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "id": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Identifier of the collection to remove."
    },
    "skill": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The skill this collection ships with, when it is skill-scoped."
    },
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "id",
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

### collections_get

Read one collection's declaration.

Read one collection: its fields, their types, and the hooks that
normalise a record on write.

A skill-scoped collection needs its Skill back to resolve — collections_list
reports it on every entry, so a caller that listed first already has it.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "id": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Identifier of the collection."
    },
    "skill": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The skill this collection ships with, when it is skill-scoped. Required to resolve one — collections_list reports it on every entry."
    },
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "id",
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

